    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.surround\n\n### Description\n\nI have really wanted to move to `mini.surround` for a while especially to have a solidly built, Lua based surround plugin. The only thing missing that is keeping me back from switching over is a proper implementation of the semantics surround count as it pertains to the vim grammar.\r\n\r\nIn the grammar of a vim command you have `[count1][operator][count2][object]` as the basic structure and `count1` and `count2` are not necessarily equal. As  in, there is a difference semantically between `3dw` and `d3w`. The first says \"3 times, delete 1 word\" and the second says \"1 time, delete 3 words\". So if you combine them `3d3w` is saying \"3 times delete 3 words\" which in a lot of cases like deleting is equivalent to `9dw` or `d9w`, but semantically they are different operations and are handled inside of vim in a different way.\r\n\r\nWhen it comes to doing surround operations `count1` should apply to the `operator` and `count2` should apply to the `object`. This is not how it's currently implemented in `mini.surround` where `2saw(` converts `foo bar` to `(foo bar)` so it is applying the 2 to the text object and not the operator. In the vim grammar this is actually saying \"2 times, add a surround around 1 word\", so it should be `2saw((` and convert `foo bar` to `((foo)) bar`.\r\n\r\nWith this structure you also are able to express stuff much more complicated something like `3sa2w([{` would convert `foo bar` to `{[(foo bar)]}`.\r\n\r\nThis would be a super killer feature and would make `mini.surround` the first Lua based surround plugin to implement the semantics of their commands in proper alignment with vim methodology.\r\n\r\n**References**\r\n\r\n- `vim-sandwich` documentation on how count works in their operators: https://github.com/machakann/vim-sandwich/blob/master/doc/operator-sandwich.txt#L142-L164\r\n- Very good blog post covering Vim Grammar and the structuring of commands with operations/motions/objects and also covers how counts work with these: https://takac.github.io/2013/01/30/vim-grammar/",
