            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Soo...\r\n\r\nI did try to add support for this and almost gave up. Mostly because the following is not the case:\r\n\r\n> So if you combine them 3d3w is saying \"3 times delete 3 words\" which in a lot of cases like deleting is equivalent to 9dw or d9w, but semantically they are different operations and are handled inside of vim in a different way.\r\n\r\nI knew that from plugin author point of view they are the same because `:h v:count` says so. But after discovering `:h motion-count-multiplied`, it seems that `3d3w` **is** the same as `9dw` or `d9w`.\r\n\r\nThe main problem was that I couldn't find a way to \"reset\" `v:count` after obtaining `[count1]` in `[count1][operator][count2][textobject/motion]` which matters because Neovim has to place marks according to `[count2]` and not `[count1] * [count2]`. This is where I started writing why this won't be supported, but decided to give it one last go.\r\n\r\nAnd it was successful. `v:count` is reset if another command is executed which is a problem in itself, because this is not exactly allowed in Operator-pending mode (where it is needed here). So I came up with [this black magic hackery](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/a2bafb341894044e887e209fc013bf955371f9ae/lua/mini/surround.lua#L1224) which almost didn't break anything. There were slight hiccups afterwards (proper clearing cache and dot-repeat support), but seems to work nicely. Not sure if flawlessly, but nicely.\r\n\r\n@mehalter, thanks for the push and interesting hacking problem! If you find anything which don't work in this use case, let me know.\r\n\r\n(Side note: looking at 'vim-sandwich' code did nothing for me, as it seems to handle `[count]` completely by itself without relying on Vim's built-in ways)",
